import{_ as t,o,c as a,ao as d}from"./chunks/framework.D5egYY-M.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Detailed capabilities and limits","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"capabilities.md","filePath":"capabilities.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"capabilities.md"};function i(r,e,n,c,l,g){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="Detailed-capabilities-and-limits" tabindex="-1">Detailed capabilities and limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#Detailed-capabilities-and-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Detailed capabilities and limits {#Detailed-capabilities-and-limits}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Use this page <strong>after</strong> choosing a scientific route, not as a first tutorial. For a first distributional model, start with <a href="./getting-started">Getting started</a>. For a tree, start with <a href="./tutorials/phylogenetic-models">Phylogenetic structured effects</a>. For Gaussian meta-analysis with supplied sampling variances, start with <a href="./tutorials/meta-analysis">Mean effects and residual heterogeneity</a>.</p><p>This is the detailed map of what <code>DRModels.jl</code> can fit today. It is deliberately conservative: “tested” means the route has numerical checks for the listed use. It does not imply a complete worked analysis for every route or calibrated interval coverage; the rows identify those limits. “Implemented, untested” means it is not yet a recommended analysis route; “not available” means the request cannot currently be fit with DRModels.jl.</p><p>Status legend:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Tested</strong>: numerical checks exist for the listed use; read the row&#39;s worked-analysis and inference limits before reporting results.</p></li><li><p><strong>Impl, untested</strong> — not yet a recommended analysis route.</p></li><li><p><strong>Not available</strong> — this request cannot currently be fit with DRModels.jl.</p></li></ul><p>Use <a href="./model-guides/model-map">What can I fit today?</a> for a model-building route, and <a href="./getting-started">Getting started</a> for runnable syntax.</p><h2 id="Response-families" tabindex="-1">Response families <a class="header-anchor" href="#Response-families" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Response families {#Response-families}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each family below can be fitted with fixed effects. The family-level tests use simulated data with known parameters; comparisons with the optional R bridge are run separately.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Family</th><th style="text-align:right;">Mean-axis random effects</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, correlated, crossed, and structured effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; crossed fits are fast at moderate sizes but still slower than drmTMB at tens of thousands of rows (see <a href="./model-guides/large-data">Working with large data</a>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Student-t</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts and slopes</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; independent <code>(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)</code> terms are checked against drmTMB 0.8.1 without <code>sd()</code> formulas; with <code>sd()</code> formulas they fit but drmTMB 0.8.1 errors on that shape, so there is no reference. A coordinate <code>spatial(1 | g, coords = …)</code> or <code>spatial(1 + x | g, coords = …)</code> term (no <code>sd()</code> formula, <code>sigma ~ 1</code>, <code>nu ~ 1</code>) uses drmTMB&#39;s fixed-range kernel and the TMB-convention Laplace; the <code>+ x</code> form is the independent intercept and slope. Checked against drmTMB 0.8.1 on the gap-map data (<code>test/test_student_spatial_coords.jl</code>). Other spatial shapes (<code>sd()</code> formulas, labelled correlated blocks, <code>sigma</code> or <code>nu</code> covariates) are still refused</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">SkewNormal</td><td style="text-align:right;">—</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> fixed effects; random effects are refused</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Poisson</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; independent `(1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">NegBinomial2</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; independent `(1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">TruncatedNegBinomial2</td><td style="text-align:right;">—</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> fixed effects</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">TruncatedPoisson</td><td style="text-align:right;">—</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> fixed effects</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Beta</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; crossed random effects have numerical tests but no complete worked analysis; independent `(1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">BetaBinomial</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; constant <code>sigma</code> only; precision cap on crossed and structured routes (see the note below the table)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Binomial</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, correlated slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; correlated <code>(1 + x | g)</code> is admitted; independent `(1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gamma</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, crossed, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; independent intercept and slope terms with an <code>sd()</code> formula per term (checked against drmTMB 0.8.1 for two terms; drmTMB errors on three or four, so those fits have no reference); crossed random effects have numerical tests but no complete worked analysis; shape cap on crossed and structured routes (see the note below the table)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LogNormal</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, slopes, phylogenetic, and known-matrix effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; independent <code>(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)</code> terms (exact Gaussian marginal of <code>log(y)</code>, agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1; the test asserts logLik within 1e-4, and the tighter gaps quoted in the design note come from <code>tools/nongauss_indep_compare.jl</code> output that is not committed); <code>animal</code> and coordinate-spatial effects are refused; a correlated <code>(1 + x | g)</code> block takes SD formulas on one or both coefficients, q = 2 only (logLik agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1 to 1e-9)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">ZeroOneBeta</td><td style="text-align:right;">mean (<code>mu</code>) random intercepts, <code>(1 | g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; <code>sigma</code>, <code>zoi</code> and <code>coi</code> stay fixed-effects-only; <code>coi</code> is not identified unless the data have both exact 0s and exact 1s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Tweedie</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, independent slopes, and a single <code>phylo(1 | g)</code> or <code>relmat(1 | g)</code> mean intercept (optionally with <code>sd(g, level) ~ z</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">Checked against drmTMB 0.8.1 on 10 pre-registered fits (5 simulated datasets, <code>sd(g, level) ~ 1</code> and <code>~ z</code>): 9 of 10 agree (largest coefficient difference 5.6e-7), and the tenth (phylo, <code>sd ~ z</code>) is R&#39;s worse optimum, 2.7 log-likelihood units below Julia&#39;s; R&#39;s standard error for the bare-<code>sd</code> intercept came back non-finite in the comparison, so the standard-error check is incomplete. Structured slopes and structured <code>q &gt;= 2</code> blocks are refused, as in drmTMB 0.8.1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">CumulativeLogit (ordinal)</td><td style="text-align:right;">intercepts, independent slopes, and phylogenetic effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Underdispersed counts (variance below the mean)</td><td style="text-align:right;">—</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Not available</strong>: no Conway–Maxwell–Poisson or generalised Poisson family. Use glmmTMB&#39;s <code>compois()</code> or <code>genpois()</code> families</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Censored responses (left-, right- or interval-censored values)</td><td style="text-align:right;">—</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Not available</strong> in any family; detection limits or censored survival times cannot be modelled here. Use a package with censored likelihoods, such as brms (<code>y | cens(censored) ~ …</code>) or the survival package</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Dispersion cap on crossed and structured routes</p><p>With crossed random effects or a structured effect (<code>phylo</code>, <code>relmat</code>) on the mean, NegBinomial2, Gamma, Beta and BetaBinomial fits cap the precision-type parameter (NB2 size, Gamma shape, Beta precision) at <code>exp(8)</code> ≈ 2981. On the <code>sigma</code> scale this is a floor of <code>exp(-4)</code> ≈ 0.018. Data that need a smaller <code>sigma</code> can stop at the cap: Gamma data with a coefficient of variation below about 1.8%, very tightly concentrated proportions, or nearly Poisson counts with large means. The raw optimiser flag can still be true at the cap; this is not evidence that the optimum lies inside the permitted range. The fit warns when it ends at a likelihood clamp. If you see that warning, or your fitted <code>sigma</code> is at 0.018, treat the dispersion estimate as a bound, not an estimate, and compare with the same model without the random effect or with drmTMB, which uses a wider cap. The cap itself is unchanged; see <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/DRModels.jl/issues/959" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DRModels.jl#959</a>. Ordinary <code>(1 | g)</code> random intercepts and fixed-effect fits do not use this cap.</p></div><p><strong>Count modifiers</strong> <code>zi</code> (zero-inflation) and <code>hu</code> (hurdle): implemented in the Poisson/NB2 paths; <strong>Tested</strong>.</p><p><strong>Beta boundary modifiers</strong> <code>zoi</code> / <code>coi</code> (zero-/one-inflation): the <code>ZeroOneBeta()</code> family handles the boundary mass; <strong>Tested</strong> when the data contain both exact zeros and exact ones. With only one kind, <code>coi</code> sits at its boundary while the fit still reports convergence; an ordered-beta family is not available (<a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/DRModels.jl/issues/975" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DRModels.jl#975</a>).</p><h2 id="Distributional-location–scale-sub-models" tabindex="-1">Distributional (location–scale) sub-models <a class="header-anchor" href="#Distributional-location–scale-sub-models" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Distributional (location–scale) sub-models {#Distributional-location–scale-sub-models}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A formula per distributional parameter is the core grammar: <code>bf(...)</code> gives the mean, scale, and any additional parameter supported by the chosen family their own formulas.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian mean μ and scale σ formulas</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; both intercepts and slopes are recovered.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Non-Gaussian <code>sigma</code> or dispersion formula</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for families with a dispersion model.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Student-t <code>nu</code> (degrees of freedom) formula</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Random effect on the Gaussian scale axis, <code>sigma ~ (1|g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>. By default each group&#39;s effect is integrated by 32-node Gauss–Hermite quadrature. That is close to exact for small groups but loses accuracy as groups grow. In our checks with 150 to 400 rows per group, a default fit&#39;s log-likelihood differed from drmTMB&#39;s by up to about 4 units, sometimes above and sometimes below, and in two of those checks its random-effect SD estimate was about twice drmTMB&#39;s. Pass <code>marginal = :Laplace</code> to use the Laplace approximation that drmTMB uses; it then gives the same log-likelihood, estimates and standard errors as drmTMB, and stays within 0.01 units of the exact value at those group sizes. Use it for large groups or when you compare results with drmTMB. <code>:Laplace</code> needs a fixed-effect mean and maximum likelihood; other models refuse it. <code>lrtest</code> accepts a <code>:Laplace</code> fit against the fixed-effect model <code>sigma ~ 1</code>, but refuses to compare it with a default-integrator fit of a random-effect model. Parametric bootstrap now redraws the Gaussian scale-axis random intercept, including when a mean random effect is also present. This repairs the replicate generator; it does not establish interval coverage for the random-effect SD. Inspect refit diagnostics and keep inference claims within directly checked model cells.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Random effects on the scale axis beyond that single Gaussian intercept: LogNormal, Gamma, NegBinomial2 and BetaBinomial <code>sigma ~ x + (1|g)</code>; Gaussian <code>sigma ~ x + (0 + x|g)</code>, <code>(1 + x|g)</code>, <code>(1|g) + (1|h)</code>, and a mean <code>(1|g)</code> with <code>sigma ~ x + (1|h)</code> on a different group</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> against drmTMB 0.8.1 (eight fits on one simulated dataset: log-likelihood within 1e-4, fixed effects within 1e-3 of a standard error, log random-effect SDs within 5e-3). The Laplace approximation only, with a fixed-effect mean except for the Gaussian mean-plus-sigma pair. <code>fitted</code> and <code>residuals</code> use the population-level mean and sigma (random effects set to zero), so quantile residuals are not N(0, 1) when the scale random effects are large. Other shapes are refused by name.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sigma(fit)</code> and <code>corpairs(fit)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> post-fit accessors.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Location–scale–scale-models-LSS,-sd" tabindex="-1">Location–scale–scale models (LSS, <code>sd()</code>) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Location–scale–scale-models-LSS,-sd" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Location–scale–scale models (LSS, `sd()`) {#Location–scale–scale-models-LSS,-sd}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A third submodel can put a linear predictor on the log standard deviation of a random effect: <code>sd(group) ~ z</code> or <code>sd(species, phylogenetic) ~ z</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Plain IID LSS <code>sd(group) ~ z</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> under ML and opt-in REML.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Phylogenetic LSS <code>sd(species, phylogenetic) ~ z</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> under ML and opt-in REML; large phylogenetic fits use the sparse engine.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Sparse phylogenetic LSS</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested.</strong> Request <code>sparse = true</code> or <code>algorithm = :sparse_lbfgs</code>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Multi-component LSS</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for multi-IID and IID-plus-phylogenetic structures.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Incomplete responses</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for <code>missing</code> or <code>NaN</code> outcomes on LSS routes.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Random-effect-structures" tabindex="-1">Random-effect structures <a class="header-anchor" href="#Random-effect-structures" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Random-effect structures {#Random-effect-structures}&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="Plain-unstructured-random-effects-on-the-mean" tabindex="-1">Plain (unstructured) random effects on the mean <a class="header-anchor" href="#Plain-unstructured-random-effects-on-the-mean" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plain (unstructured) random effects on the mean {#Plain-unstructured-random-effects-on-the-mean}&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Structure</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian random intercept <code>(1|g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian random slope <code>(x|g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Correlated intercept and slope</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Multiple, crossed, or nested grouping factors</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; Gaussian crossed factors fit in about 0.3 s at 2,000 rows and 45 s at 20,000 rows on one synthetic design, still slower than drmTMB at tens of thousands of rows (see <a href="./model-guides/large-data">Working with large data</a>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Poisson crossed random effects</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> with sparse Laplace integration</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian correlated 2-coefficient blocks beside random slope terms: <code>(1 + x | g) + (0 + z | h)</code>, two blocks <code>(1 + x | g) + (1 + z | h)</code>, a block beside <code>(1 | h) + (0 + z | h)</code>, with optional <code>sd(·, coef(·)) ~ …</code> formulas</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> by exact dense maximum likelihood (ML only; 5,000-row cap) against drmTMB 0.8.1 at the optimum on 21 fits over three datasets. Refused by name: an <code>sd()</code> formula on a grouping that carries a block and another term (drmTMB refuses it), a 3- or 4-coefficient block beside a slope term (drmTMB&#39;s own fit does not converge), two blocks when either has 3 or more coefficients (not checked), two blocks on one grouping, and a label shared by two terms (drmTMB ignores it).</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gaussian mean <code>sd(g) ~ …</code> with a <code>sigma</code> random intercept: independent <code>(1 | g)</code> in <code>mu</code> and <code>sigma ~ 1 + (1 | h)</code>, or the labelled pair <code>(1 | p | g)</code> in both</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> by dense Laplace against drmTMB 0.8.1 (9 fits over three datasets, log-likelihood within 5e-9). ML only; <code>sigma</code> random slopes and REML are refused by name.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="Structured-random-effects-with-a-known-relatedness-matrix-closed-form-Gaussian" tabindex="-1">Structured random effects with a known relatedness matrix (closed-form Gaussian) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Structured-random-effects-with-a-known-relatedness-matrix-closed-form-Gaussian" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Structured random effects with a known relatedness matrix (closed-form Gaussian) {#Structured-random-effects-with-a-known-relatedness-matrix-closed-form-Gaussian}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A structured intercept <code>u ~ N(0, σ_s² K)</code> keeps the Gaussian marginal exactly Gaussian and is fit in closed form (PGLS / matrix-determinant lemma).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Marker</th><th style="text-align:right;">Supplies</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>relmat(1|id)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">user matrix <code>K</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>animal(1|id)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">additive-relatedness <code>A</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>phylo(1|species)</code> on the <strong>mean</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">tree (<code>AugmentedPhy</code> or Newick)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>spatial(1|site)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">coordinates; <code>K(ρ)=exp(-d/ρ)</code>, with ρ estimated</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; drmTMB&#39;s <code>spatial()</code> fixes ρ at the median pairwise distance instead, so the two packages fit different models from the same formula</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>temporal(1|id, time, ar1)</code> / <code>temporal(1|id, time, ou)</code> on the <strong>mean</strong> (drmTMB development version: <code>temporal(1 | id, time = …, structure = &quot;ar1&quot;/&quot;ou&quot;)</code>, not yet released)</td><td style="text-align:right;">an integer occasion (AR1) or numeric elapsed-time (OU) column</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (<code>test/test_temporal_ar1.jl</code>, <code>test/test_temporal_ou.jl</code>): dense-oracle likelihood to 1e-10, recovery, and a GLLVModels.jl logLik cross-check to 1e-13. ML, <code>sigma ~ 1</code>, optional same-id <code>(1|id)</code> only; other families, <code>sigma</code> terms, slopes, REML and other structured terms are refused by name. <code>fitted</code>/<code>predict</code> are population-level (drmTMB&#39;s <code>fitted</code> is conditional; conditional effects are in <code>ranef</code>); <code>simulate</code>/<code>bootstrap_ci</code> draw a fresh temporal chain. Wald SEs and intervals are reported for every coordinate (drmTMB: AR1 mean coefficients only; OU Wald refused), and <code>bootstrap_ci</code> covers every coordinate (drmTMB refuses the temporal bootstrap); these are extensions with no interval calibration claimed. drmTMB parity: 8 cells (AR1 and OU, with and without <code>(1|id)</code>, including the drmTMB article&#39;s two datasets) matched logLik to ≤ 1e-11 and every estimate to ≤ 1e-8 relative in the measured run (Julia 1.12.6, Linux, Totoro); the tests enforce 1e-8 absolute (logLik) and 1e-6 relative (<code>test/test_parity_temporal.jl</code>; worked example: <a href="./tutorials/temporal-random-effects">Temporal AR1, OU and Toeplitz effects</a>).</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>phylo(1|species) + temporal(1|species, elapsed, ou)</code> on the <strong>mean</strong>, <code>drm(...; tree)</code> (drmTMB development version: <code>phylo(1 | species, tree = tree) + temporal(1 | species, time = elapsed, structure = &quot;ou&quot;)</code>, not yet released)</td><td style="text-align:right;">an <strong>ultrametric</strong> Newick tree (or <code>AugmentedPhy</code>) whose tips are exactly the observed species; zero-length branches allowed; numeric elapsed time</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (<code>test/test_temporal_phylo_ou.jl</code>): additive phylogenetic stable intercept (tip-correlation scale, as drmTMB) + independent OU path per species + noise; dense-oracle likelihood ≤ 9.2e-13 (random θ, decay and stable-SD extremes), zero-length branches and the pruning pass on non-ultrametric trees exact to 1e-10, dense-oracle modes, recovery smoke, full-model <code>simulate</code> (Julia 1.10.12). drmTMB&#39;s rules are refused by name (OU only, same grouping, no <code>(1|species)</code>, ≥ 3 species with ≥ 2 times, ≥ 3 lags, tips = species, ultrametric tree). drmTMB parity: 2 cells (the drmTMB commit is recorded in each cell&#39;s <code>expected.meta.toml</code>) — logLik ≤ 5.5e-12, estimates ≤ 2.1e-11 relative, conditional fitted ≤ 2.3e-12 (Julia 1.10.12). No interval claim: profile <code>confint</code> warns that the intervals are uncalibrated, as drmTMB&#39;s does. drmTMB refuses Wald and bootstrap intervals for this fit; DRModels.jl returns them as an extension, without a calibration claim, and does not warn that they are uncalibrated. Temporal boundary diagnostic in <code>check_drm(fit).temporal_boundary</code>. Worked example: <a href="./tutorials/temporal-random-effects">Temporal AR1, OU and Toeplitz effects</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>temporal(1|id, occ, homtoep)</code> on the <strong>mean</strong> (drmTMB development version: <code>temporal(1 | id, time = occ, structure = &quot;homtoep&quot;)</code>, not yet released)</td><td style="text-align:right;">a complete panel: every <code>id</code> observed at the same 3–12 equally spaced integer occasions, at least as many ids as occasions (missing responses dropped first, as drmTMB)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (<code>test/test_temporal_homtoep.jl</code>): within-series covariance σ²R, R a free positive-definite Toeplitz correlation matrix parameterised by partial autocorrelations (Durbin–Levinson); σ is the TOTAL SD and, as in drmTMB, no process SD, residual SD or <code>(1|id)</code> (not identified). BigFloat dense-oracle likelihood ≤ 4e-15 relative (partial autocorrelations up to ±0.995) and a 2048-bit Yule–Walker reference ≤ 3e-14 at atanh PACs up to ±40, dense Schur check of the parameterisation, recovery, <code>simulate</code> lag covariances, whitened residuals (<code>type = :quantile</code>) against a dense Cholesky (Julia 1.10.12). drmTMB&#39;s panel rules refused by name. <strong>Inference scope:</strong> as drmTMB, mean-coefficient profile intervals; <code>vcov</code>, <code>stderror</code>, Wald <code>confint</code>, <code>predict(se = true)</code> and profiles of σ or the lag correlations are refused (<code>confint</code> and <code>profile_curve</code>, as drmTMB&#39;s <code>profile()</code>; <code>parameter_surface</code> too, which has no drmTMB counterpart); <code>coeftable</code> shows <code>NaN</code> SEs. As an extension (drmTMB refuses the temporal bootstrap), <code>bootstrap_ci</code> / <code>bootstrap_summary</code> / <code>bootstrap_result</code> give mean-coefficient percentile intervals, without a calibration claim. drmTMB parity: 3 cells (the drmTMB commit is recorded in each cell&#39;s <code>expected.meta.toml</code>) — logLik ≤ 9.6e-12, lag correlations ≤ 4.9e-10, Pearson residuals ≤ 1e-6, profile endpoints ≤ 6.6e-6 (Julia 1.10.12). No calibration claim of its own. Worked example: <a href="./tutorials/temporal-random-effects">Temporal AR1, OU and Toeplitz effects</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">One <code>phylo</code>/<code>relmat</code>/<code>animal</code> marker <strong>plus</strong> ordinary <code>(1|h)</code> or <code>(0 + x|h)</code> bars on the mean</td><td style="text-align:right;">tree / <code>K</code> / <code>A</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>, ML only; matches drmTMB <code>engine = &quot;tmb&quot;</code> on nine test datasets. Independent blocks; the marker SD is on the correlation scale. (Other routes for the same <code>phylo</code> marker can report the raw branch-length scale; see the <code>meta_V</code> row below and <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/DRModels.jl/issues/979" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DRModels.jl#979</a>.) REML, <code>(1 + x|h)</code>, range-estimated <code>spatial()</code>, <code>penalty</code> and sparse algorithms are refused by name. With <code>meta_V(v)</code> added, the <code>meta_V</code> row below fits it.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Gaussian table above describes the simple intercept route. It does not rule out the supported non-Gaussian phylogenetic mean models or the more specific phylogenetic location–scale routes below; those routes have their own family and inference boundaries.</p><p>A Beta <code>relmat()</code> intercept uses the supplied <code>K</code> as given, as native drmTMB does and as Julia&#39;s closed-form Gaussian route above does: it is not rescaled to a correlation matrix. Fitting the same data with <code>K</code> and <code>2K</code> gives identical fixed effects and log-likelihood, and the random-effect SD shifts by <code>log(sqrt(2))</code>, as a raw covariance scale requires.</p><h3 id="Coordinate-spatial-spatial1site-on-non-Gaussian-means" tabindex="-1">Coordinate-spatial <code>spatial(1|site)</code> on non-Gaussian means <a class="header-anchor" href="#Coordinate-spatial-spatial1site-on-non-Gaussian-means" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coordinate-spatial `spatial(1|site)` on non-Gaussian means {#Coordinate-spatial-spatial1site-on-non-Gaussian-means}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Only the Gaussian mean route estimates the spatial range. Every route below fixes the kernel at drmTMB&#39;s native <code>exp(-d / m) + 1e-6 I</code>, with <code>m</code> the median positive pairwise distance between sites, so the same data and formula give the same covariance as native drmTMB.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Family</th><th style="text-align:right;">Accepts</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Poisson</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>coords</code> (native kernel, range fixed); optional <code>method = :REML</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1 on the wave-10 cell: log-likelihood to 2e-9, fixed effects within 1e-5 R standard errors, log SD to 2e-7 (<code>test/test_spatial_parity_wave10.jl</code>). Before wave 10 this route estimated the range, which gave a log-likelihood 1.23 higher and a sixth parameter; with <code>sd(site, spatial) ~ z</code> the SD fit also agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1 (<code>test/test_struct_sd_counts.jl</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gamma</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>coords</code> (native kernel, 1-D vector allowed) or a supplied <code>K</code>, not both; with or without <code>sd(site, spatial) ~ z</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; a <code>coords</code> fit equals the same fit with the native kernel passed as <code>K</code> (log-likelihoods agree to 1e-12 on one simulated dataset)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">NegBinomial2</td><td style="text-align:right;">a supplied <code>K</code> on <code>spatial(1 | site)</code>; <code>coords</code> (native kernel) with <code>sd(site, spatial) ~ z</code> on <code>spatial(1 | site)</code> or on a slope-only <code>spatial(0 + x | site)</code> term</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the intercept SD fit agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1. A bare <code>spatial(1 | site)</code> with <code>coords</code> and no SD formula is still refused; a supplied <code>K</code> together with an <code>sd()</code> formula is refused, and that message calls the route a slope route even for <code>spatial(1 | site)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Beta</td><td style="text-align:right;">a supplied <code>K</code> on <code>spatial(1 | site)</code> (no SD formula); <code>coords</code> (native kernel) on a bare <code>spatial(1 | site)</code> or <code>spatial(1 + x | site)</code> (completed with constant coefficient SDs, as drmTMB 0.8.1 fits them) or with <code>sd(site, spatial) ~ z</code> or the independent <code>spatial(1 + x | site)</code> block with one <code>sd(site, spatial, coef(·))</code> formula per coefficient</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; <code>K</code> is refused when an SD formula is present, and <code>sigma ~ 1</code> is required</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Binomial</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>coords</code> with an <code>sd(site, spatial)</code> formula, or a bare <code>spatial(1 | site)</code> (completed to <code>sd(site, spatial) ~ 1</code>, as drmTMB 0.8.1 fits it; drmTMB flags that fit as not converged on the gap-map data)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; this route keeps the older fixed range (mean pairwise distance, 1e-8 ridge) because drmTMB has no Binomial spatial route</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LogNormal, Tweedie</td><td style="text-align:right;">not accepted</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>spatial</code> is refused by name</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="Structured-and-positive-family-SD-cells-added-after-the-wave-10-gap-map" tabindex="-1">Structured and positive-family SD cells added after the wave-10 gap map <a class="header-anchor" href="#Structured-and-positive-family-SD-cells-added-after-the-wave-10-gap-map" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Structured and positive-family SD cells added after the wave-10 gap map {#Structured-and-positive-family-SD-cells-added-after-the-wave-10-gap-map}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each row was compared with drmTMB 0.8.1 on the committed 400-row gap-map datasets (<code>test/test_struct_sd_counts.jl</code>; log-likelihood to 1e-4, every coefficient to 1e-3 and 0.02 R-SE, SEs to 1%).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Cell</th><th style="text-align:right;">Route</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status and boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">NegBinomial2 <code>phylo(1 | g)</code> with <code>sd(g, phylogenetic) ~ z</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">tip correlation of an ultrametric <code>tree</code>, dense Laplace; <code>sigma ~ 1</code> required</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gamma <code>(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)</code> with one <code>sd(g, coef(.)) ~ z</code> formula</td><td style="text-align:right;">the other coefficient keeps a constant SD (the default <code>~ 1</code>); the two-field engine</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for Gamma only; Poisson and NB2 pairs are not covered here</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LogNormal <code>(1 | g) + (1 | h)</code> with <code>sd(g)</code> and <code>sd(h)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">log y is Gaussian: the Gaussian route plus the Jacobian</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the labelled <code>(1 + x | g)</code> pair with two coefficient SDs is reached the same way but is not checked against drmTMB (no committed R reference cell and no test covers it)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Student <code>(1 | g) + (1 | h)</code> with <code>sd(g)</code> and <code>sd(h)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">crossed sparse Laplace with a log-SD regression per factor; <code>sigma ~ 1</code>, <code>nu ~ 1</code> required</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Binomial <code>relmat(1 + x | g)</code> with coefficient SDs</td><td style="text-align:right;">refused</td><td style="text-align:right;">R does not converge on these cells (<code>is_converged()</code> is FALSE, an SD at -10.7), so they stay refused</td></tr></tbody></table><p>NegBinomial2 <code>spatial(1 + x \\| p \\| site)</code> (the labelled, correlated intercept-and-slope block) with an <code>sd(site, spatial, coef(·)) ~ z</code> formula per coefficient has no drmTMB 0.8.1 counterpart (drmTMB refuses the labelled form). The fit starts from the default point and from four tilts of both SD blocks and keeps the best optimum, because the likelihood has a second interior mode that differs by the sign of the z coefficient of the intercept SD. The independent form <code>spatial(1 + x \\| site)</code> is not restarted: it agrees with drmTMB, whose default start stops at the same value.</p><h3 id="Non-Gaussian-phylogenetic-random-intercept-on-the-mean-sparse-Laplace" tabindex="-1">Non-Gaussian phylogenetic random intercept on the mean (sparse Laplace) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Non-Gaussian-phylogenetic-random-intercept-on-the-mean-sparse-Laplace" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Non-Gaussian phylogenetic random intercept on the mean (sparse Laplace) {#Non-Gaussian-phylogenetic-random-intercept-on-the-mean-sparse-Laplace}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A <code>phylo(1|species)</code> intercept on the mean for non-Gaussian families uses the verified sparse augmented-state Laplace engine.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Family</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Poisson</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">NegBinomial2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Gamma</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Binomial</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Beta</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; its gradient tolerance is deliberately looser than 1e-6</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">BetaBinomial</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; constant <code>sigma</code> only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">CumulativeLogit (ordinal)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; intercept-only</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Location–scale-with-a-phylogenetic-random-effect-on-the-scale-q2-route" tabindex="-1">Location–scale with a phylogenetic random effect on the scale (q=2 route) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Location–scale-with-a-phylogenetic-random-effect-on-the-scale-q2-route" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Location–scale with a phylogenetic random effect on the scale (q=2 route) {#Location–scale-with-a-phylogenetic-random-effect-on-the-scale-q2-route}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A shared random effect on <strong>both</strong> the mean and log-dispersion axes uses a q=2 augmented Laplace calculation with an exact derivative of its Laplace objective. The full fit, gradient, Wald summaries, and profile-likelihood intervals have regular package-test coverage.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Coupled mean and log-dispersion axes for NB2 and Gamma</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> through the public <code>drm()</code> interface</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Beta and Beta-binomial kernels</td><td style="text-align:right;">Not available through the public coupled location–scale interface</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Poisson and lognormal leaves</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Implemented, untested</strong>; do not rely on them for fitted location–scale models</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Note</p><p>For <strong>non-Gaussian</strong> responses, coupled location–scale random effects are currently available only for NB2 and Gamma. Other non-Gaussian families can place structured or ordinary random effects on the mean axis, not the scale axis.</p><p><strong>Gaussian has a separate, tested scale-phylogeny route.</strong> Write <code>bf(y ~ … + phylo(1|sp), sigma ~ phylo(1|sp))</code> for the default separate mean- and scale-phylogeny effects. A scale-only phylogeny is also supported. <code>phylo_coupled = true</code> opts into a free mean–scale phylogenetic correlation; the plain two-<code>phylo</code> syntax does not do this. Retrieve the two standard deviations with <code>gaussian_locscale_phylo_sds(fit)</code>; a coupled fit records the correlation in <code>fit.scales[:lambda_cor]</code>. On the scale-only and separate blocks, <code>profile_ci = true</code> adds profile intervals for the standard deviations; the coupled block computes none.</p><p>This Gaussian route requires a common grouping factor and one phylogenetic structured component; it cannot be combined with additional random effects or <code>meta_V</code>. <code>method = :REML</code> is available for the scale-only, separate and coupled blocks, and is refused for the iid <code>sigma ~ (1|g)</code> route. On these three blocks REML is one joint Laplace approximation over the phylogenetic effects and <strong>both</strong> the <code>mu</code> and <code>sigma</code> fixed effects, the restricted likelihood native drmTMB (<code>REML = TRUE</code>) maximises for this model, and the scale-only and coupled blocks are tested against drmTMB&#39;s REML log-likelihood, degrees of freedom and estimates. Under REML the reported <code>mu</code>/<code>sigma</code> coefficients are the joint mode at the REML variance estimates. As in drmTMB, the coupled block&#39;s mean–scale phylogenetic correlation is bounded at |cor| ≤ 0.999999. When the data put the two phylogenetic effects on one axis, the ML and REML estimates sit on that bound and are reported without a Wald covariance. Under REML, <code>profile_ci = true</code> on the scale-only and separate blocks profiles this restricted likelihood. Earlier versions profiled the ML likelihood from the REML estimate, which gave neither an ML nor a REML interval. A REML fit whose phylogenetic standard deviation is estimated at zero is reported as converged, with no Wald covariance. On the scale-only and separate blocks, <code>profile_ci = true</code> gives its <code>[0, upper]</code> interval. The coupled block computes no profile interval, under ML or REML, and currently ignores <code>profile_ci = true</code>. For other Gaussian formulas, passing <code>profile_ci = true</code> is refused unless the <code>sigma</code> formula contains <code>phylo(1 | g)</code>; to profile a fitted model outside these routes, use <code>confint(fit, method = :profile)</code> after fitting. A non-Gaussian scale-axis-only intercept is not part of the public formula grammar.</p></div><h2 id="Coevolution:-q4-phylogenetic-bivariate-location–scale-model-PLSM" tabindex="-1">Coevolution: q=4 phylogenetic bivariate location–scale model (PLSM) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Coevolution:-q4-phylogenetic-bivariate-location–scale-model-PLSM" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Coevolution: q=4 phylogenetic bivariate location–scale model (PLSM) {#Coevolution:-q4-phylogenetic-bivariate-location–scale-model-PLSM}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The selling-point model: a shared phylogenetic random effect on all four axes <code>(μ1, μ2, log σ1, log σ2)</code> with a 4×4 between-species covariance <code>Σ_a</code>, plus a residual correlation ρ12. The sparse-Laplace fit, exact sparse gradient, and public formula route are <strong>tested</strong>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>bf(mu1=…, mu2=…, sigma1=…, sigma2=…, rho12=…)</code> with phylogeny</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; recovers fixed effects and <code>Σ_a</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>relmat</code>, <code>animal</code>, and fixed-range <code>spatial</code> structured providers</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; spatial range is fixed (default: median positive pairwise distance with a 1e-6 ridge, drmTMB&#39;s native kernel; <code>spatial_range</code> overrides it)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>vc(fit)</code>, <code>ranef(fit)</code>, and <code>coevolution_cor</code> summaries</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; <code>fit.ranef.Sigma_a</code> is ordered <code>mu1, mu2, sigma1, sigma2</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Fixed-effect covariance and Wald standard errors (<code>q4_vcov=true</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>q4_f_reltol</code> (default <code>1e-7</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">The relative objective change at which the q=4 ML optimiser stops. The default can end up to about <code>1e-3</code> in log-likelihood short of the optimum on flat q=4 ridges; <code>q4_f_reltol = 1e-12</code> converges tightly at a higher run time. REML q=4 ignores it. The R bridge forwards it.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Bootstrap intervals for coevolution correlations</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested for tree-based phylogeny</strong>; non-tree structured providers return point summaries only</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Structured-q2-bivariate-Gaussian-mu1/mu2-only" tabindex="-1">Structured q=2 bivariate Gaussian (mu1/mu2 only) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Structured-q2-bivariate-Gaussian-mu1/mu2-only" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Structured q=2 bivariate Gaussian (mu1/mu2 only) {#Structured-q2-bivariate-Gaussian-mu1/mu2-only}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is a complete-response exact-Gaussian model with matching structured random intercepts on <code>mu1</code> and <code>mu2</code>. ML is the default; the public route also accepts opt-in <code>method = :REML</code>. It requires the same fixed-effect design on both mean formulas and intercept-only <code>sigma1</code>, <code>sigma2</code>, and <code>rho12</code> formulas. Current support covers point estimates and exported summaries only. It does not establish calibrated intervals, non-Gaussian q2 support, or a full R bridge workflow.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>phylo(1|species)</code> on <code>mu1</code> and <code>mu2</code>, with residual <code>rho12</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> through <code>drm()</code> and direct export</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>relmat(1|id)</code> and <code>animal(1|id)</code> on <code>mu1</code> and <code>mu2</code>, using known <code>K</code> / <code>A</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>spatial(1|site)</code> on <code>mu1</code> and <code>mu2</code>, using <code>coords</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the range is fixed, never estimated: <code>spatial_range</code> if given, else drmTMB&#39;s native kernel (median positive pairwise distance, 1e-6 ridge). An explicit <code>spatial_range</code> uses a 1e-8 ridge. The range in force is <code>fit.ranef.spatial_range</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Bivariate-and-paired-responses-residual-correlation" tabindex="-1">Bivariate and paired responses (residual correlation) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Bivariate-and-paired-responses-residual-correlation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bivariate and paired responses (residual correlation) {#Bivariate-and-paired-responses-residual-correlation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Gaussian, lognormal and Student-t routes here fit two responses jointly with a residual/scatter <code>rho12</code>; <code>associate_pairs</code> instead couples two <em>already-fitted</em> univariate models in a second stage. Only the lognormal route reaches the structured (<code>phylo</code>/<code>relmat</code>/<code>animal</code>/<code>spatial</code>) engines of the two sections above, and it does so by delegation rather than by a second engine.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Bivariate Gaussian with residual <code>rho12</code> (<code>cbind</code> / <code>mu1</code>,<code>mu2</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>rho12(fit)</code> accessor</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Bivariate <strong>lognormal</strong> (<code>drm(bf(…), LogNormal())</code>, drmTMB&#39;s <code>biv_lognormal()</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested.</strong> Both responses must be strictly positive and are modeled as bivariate normal on <code>log(Y)</code>: <code>mu1</code>/<code>mu2</code> are log-scale means and <code>rho12</code> is a log-residual correlation, not the raw-scale Pearson correlation. Structured <code>phylo</code> and <code>relmat</code> routes are tested; <code>animal</code> and <code>spatial</code> are implemented but untested on this route. <code>method = :REML</code> is refused for all bivariate LogNormal models.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Bivariate <strong>Student-t</strong> (<code>drm(bf(…, nu = …), Student())</code>, drmTMB&#39;s <code>biv_student()</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested.</strong> <code>sigma1</code>/<code>sigma2</code> are scale parameters, not marginal SDs; <code>rho12</code> is a scatter correlation; and <code>nu = 2 + exp(η)</code>, so <code>nu &gt; 2</code>. One <code>nu</code> is shared by the two responses (it may vary by row via <code>nu ~ x</code>); zero <code>rho12</code> does not imply independence at finite <code>nu</code>. This is a residual-only model: <code>phylo</code>, <code>relmat</code>, <code>animal</code>, <code>spatial</code>, and <code>method = :REML</code> are deliberately refused.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Staged pair association</strong> — <code>associate_pairs</code> / <code>latent_normal</code> / <code>association</code> / <code>PairAssociation</code> / <code>integration_diagnostics</code> (drmTMB&#39;s <code>associate_pairs()</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested.</strong> This is a two-stage, frozen-margin estimator, not a joint model. It supports <code>gaussian_bernoulli</code>, <code>gaussian_nbinom2</code>, <code>bernoulli_bernoulli</code>, <code>bernoulli_nbinom2</code>, and <code>nbinom2_nbinom2</code>; integration diagnostics are available where numerical integration is used. Its uncertainty ignores margin-estimation error, and it offers no simultaneous association bands or profile intervals. The kernel must be explicit; only <code>association ~ 1</code> is supported; <code>marginal = :AGHQ</code>, non-converged margins, non-Bernoulli binomial margins, and other pair classes are refused.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Cross-family bivariate (different families on <code>y1</code> vs <code>y2</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Experimental.</strong> <code>drm(bf(...), (Gaussian(), Poisson()); data = …)</code> uses a latent-scale scalar correlation in <code>fit.rho_latent</code>. It has narrow documented evidence and no interval-coverage claim. <code>rho12 ~ x</code> is refused: this route fits a scalar latent correlation, not an observation-specific residual correlation. See <a href="./model-guides/cross-family-methods">Cross-family methods</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Meta-analysis" tabindex="-1">Meta-analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#Meta-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Meta-analysis {#Meta-analysis}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gaussian()</code> + <code>meta_V(v)</code> with <strong>known diagonal</strong> sampling variances; τ on the σ intercept</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>meta_V(v)</code> plus random intercepts on the mean: <code>(1 | study)</code>, <code>phylo(1 | sp)</code>, <code>relmat(1 | id)</code>, <code>animal(1 | id)</code>, and sums of these with distinct grouping columns; <code>sigma ~ x</code> allowed</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (ML). Fits the same model as drmTMB <code>engine = &quot;tmb&quot;</code>: on seven comparison fits the log-likelihoods agree within 3e-10 and the estimates within 3e-9 (relative). A phylo SD is on the raw branch-length scale (× √height = drmTMB&#39;s). Which scale a phylo SD is reported on depends on the route: Gaussian <code>phylo</code> on the mean reports the correlation scale for every <code>sigma</code> formula on an ultrametric tree (<a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/DRModels.jl/issues/979" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DRModels.jl#979</a>), but this <code>meta_V</code> route reports the raw scale. Not available: REML, random slopes, <code>spatial()</code>, a <code>sigma</code> random effect, <code>sd(g) ~ …</code>, two fields on one grouping column, missing responses; bootstrap with more than one field refuses.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Bivariate known sampling covariance (<code>meta_vcov_bivariate</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Dense known sampling covariance <code>V = V</code> on the <strong>univariate</strong> Gaussian route (drmTMB&#39;s <code>meta_V(V = V)</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Not available</strong>, by design (#974). <code>drm(f, Gaussian(); V = V)</code> throws an <code>ArgumentError</code>. Use the bivariate <code>meta_vcov_bivariate</code> route (wide <code>mu1</code>/<code>mu2</code> form), or <code>meta_V(vi)</code> for known diagonal sampling variances.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">R’s deprecated <code>meta_known_V</code> alias</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Not available</strong> in Julia; use <code>meta_V</code> for known diagonal sampling variances.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Inference" tabindex="-1">Inference <a class="header-anchor" href="#Inference" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Inference {#Inference}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Method</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Wald SEs + CIs (observed information)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Profile-likelihood CIs (<code>profile_result</code>, <code>confint(:profile)</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Parametric bootstrap (<code>bootstrap_ci</code>/<code>_summary</code>/<code>_result</code>, serial + threaded)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">REML for fixed-effect Gaussian location–scale and Gaussian mean <code>(1 | g)</code>, slope-only <code>(0 + x | g)</code>, one correlated intercept/slope <code>(1 + x | g)</code>, several independent scalar terms (including crossed <code>(1 | g) + (1 | g2)</code>), <code>relmat(1 | g)</code> / <code>animal(1 | g)</code> and <code>phylo(1 + x | g)</code> (constant <code>sigma</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the new routes agree with drmTMB 0.8.1 <code>REML = TRUE</code> on three simulated datasets per route (logLik to 1e-4); the correlated-slope cell is a single restricted route</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">REML for a Gaussian correlated block of 2 to 4 coefficients <code>(1 + x1 + x2 [+ x3] | g)</code> (labelled or not) alone or beside crossed intercepts <code>+ (1 | g2)</code>, and for <code>relmat</code> / <code>animal</code> / <code>spatial(1 | g)</code> with <code>sd(g, &lt;level&gt;) ~ 1</code> or <code>~ z</code>, and the bare unlabelled <code>relmat</code> / <code>animal</code> / <code>spatial(1 + x | g)</code> (constant <code>sigma</code>, no <code>sd()</code> formula on the blocks)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for the ordinary, crossed and <code>sd(g, &lt;level&gt;)</code> shapes: agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1 <code>REML = TRUE</code> on three simulated datasets per shape (logLik to 1e-4), except ordinary q = 4 seed 22, where neither R nor Julia converges and the cell rests on two datasets. The labelled forms have an R comparison on one shape only (ordinary q = 3). The bare structured q = 2 cells have no R reference: they are completed to the explicit <code>sd() ~ 1</code> form and tested only for equality with it</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>reml_loglik</code> / <code>ml_loglik</code> / <code>estimation_method</code> accessors</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Epsilon-method bias correction (<code>bias_correct</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>χ̄² (chi-bar-square) boundary inference</strong> (Self–Liang / Stram–Lee mixture)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">REML on q=4 Laplace and Location–Scale–Scale models</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Percentile bootstrap intervals for <code>sigma</code> terms in mixed models</p><p><code>bootstrap_ci</code> returns plain percentile intervals, and for a <code>sigma</code> coefficient in a mixed model (a model with random effects) they are too narrow. In a simulated Gaussian location-scale model with a mean random intercept (95% intervals, <code>B = 199</code>, four cells of groups and random-intercept SD), the percentile interval for the <code>sigma</code> intercept covered 83 to 90% of the time, one-sidedly, while profile intervals for the same target covered 92 to 94% (#1119). The intervals are kept as they are and <code>bootstrap_ci</code> now warns when it returns them. For <code>sigma</code> terms in a mixed model, prefer <code>confint(fit, method = :profile)</code>.</p></div><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">REML scope</p><p><code>method=:REML</code> is opt-in. <strong>ML is the default</strong> (REML likelihoods are not comparable across fixed-effect structures). Supported models are: the fixed-effect Gaussian location–scale model; a single Gaussian mean intercept <code>(1 | g)</code>; one correlated Gaussian mean intercept/slope <code>(1 + x | g)</code> with no <code>sigma</code> random effect, structured term, <code>meta_V()</code>, crossed term, or additional random-effect term; and, with a constant <code>sigma</code> and no <code>sigma</code> random effect or <code>meta_V()</code>: slope-only <code>(0 + x | g)</code>, several independent scalar terms (<code>(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)</code>, crossed <code>(1 | g) + (1 | g2)</code>; at most 200 random-effect columns in total), one <code>relmat(1 | g)</code> or <code>animal(1 | g)</code>, and <code>phylo(1 + x | g)</code> with two SDs (exact restricted likelihood; agrees with drmTMB 0.8.1 <code>REML = TRUE</code> to 1e-4 on three simulated datasets per route); Location–Scale–Scale models (<code>sd(g) ~ z</code>, <code>sd(species, phylogenetic) ~ z</code>, and multi-component LSS); the bivariate q=4 location–scale engine; and the Gaussian <code>phylo(1 | g)</code>-on-<code>sigma</code> location–scale blocks (scale-only, separate, coupled), where both the <code>mu</code> and <code>sigma</code> fixed effects are integrated out with the phylogenetic effects. The structured intercept-and-slope SD blocks on the mean (<code>KIND(1 + x | g)</code> with one <code>sd(g, KIND, coef(·))</code> formula per coefficient: <code>phylo</code> labelled or not, unlabelled <code>animal</code> / <code>relmat</code> / <code>spatial</code> with <code>sigma ~ 1</code>) and the structured <code>sigma</code> intercept with an SD formula (<code>sigma ~ 1 + KIND(1 | g)</code>, <code>sd(g, KIND, sigma) ~ z</code>, fixed-effect mean; <code>beta_mu</code> and <code>beta_sigma</code> are integrated out in one joint Laplace approximation) also admit REML, as native drmTMB does. Also under REML (constant <code>sigma</code>, no <code>sd()</code> formula): one correlated block of 3 or 4 coefficients <code>(1 + x + z [+ w] | g)</code>, a correlated block of 2 to 4 coefficients beside random intercepts on other groupings <code>(1 + x | g) + (1 | h)</code> (labelled or not), a structured intercept <code>relmat</code> / <code>animal</code> / <code>spatial(1 | g)</code> with its own <code>sd(g, &lt;level&gt;) ~ …</code>, and the bare unlabelled <code>relmat</code> / <code>animal</code> / <code>spatial(1 + x | g)</code> (completed to <code>sd(·, coef(·)) ~ 1</code> for both coefficients, as under ML). Labelled <code>animal</code> / <code>relmat</code> / <code>spatial</code> q2 blocks and heteroscedastic <code>sigma</code> with those providers still refuse REML. Ordinary <code>sd()</code> q2/q3/q4 routes stay ML only (native refuses REML there too), as do the Gaussian shapes that fit by ML in wave 9 (an unstructured q3/q4 block with <code>sd()</code> formulas, a labelled q3/q4 block, a correlated block with <code>sd()</code> formulas beside random intercepts, and independent intercept and slope terms with <code>sd()</code> formulas) and their wave-10 extensions (a block beside slope terms, two blocks, a mean <code>sd(g)</code> formula with a <code>sigma</code> intercept): each refuses <code>method = :REML</code> with a message that says REML is not implemented. A single labelled q2 block <code>(1 + x | p | g)</code> is the ordinary correlated block once the label is removed, so it fits under REML like <code>(1 + x | g)</code>; no R comparison was run for that shape. Still refused under REML on the Gaussian mean routes: the bare <code>spatial(1 | g)</code> (it estimates the exponential range where drmTMB fixes it, so the two maximum likelihoods differ by design; write <code>sd(g, spatial) ~ 1</code> for drmTMB&#39;s fixed-range kernel), a correlated <code>(1 + x | g)</code> block beside a random slope term or on the same grouping as another term, ordinary σ-RE (including ordinary <code>sd(g, sigma)</code>, which native drmTMB admits under REML), a <code>sigma</code> predictor with a multi-term mean, <code>meta_V()</code>, missing responses on the new routes, and other random-slope structures. Separate opt-in Poisson Cox–Reid routes are described under <a href="/DRModels.jl/previews/PR1396/reference/model-specification#DRModels.Poisson"><code>Poisson</code></a>; their recovery evidence is limited and does not establish general non-Gaussian REML support. This is not AI-REML. The retained correlated-slope comparison covers one Gaussian <code>sleepstudy</code> point fit against lme4 and drmTMB; it does not establish route-wide recovery or interval coverage.</p><p><strong>Normalisation convention:</strong> every REML route in DRModels.jl reports a <strong>normalised</strong> restricted log-likelihood, including the <code>(n_β/2)·log(2π)</code> constant, so <code>reml_loglik</code> is directly comparable to lme4&#39;s, glmmTMB&#39;s, TMB&#39;s and drmTMB&#39;s <code>logLik()</code>. (The bivariate q=2/q=4 Laplace routes once omitted that constant; this has been corrected.) Two quantities are in use. Most routes report the Patterson–Thompson restricted log-likelihood. The Gaussian <code>phylo(1 | g)</code>-on-<code>sigma</code> location–scale blocks instead report TMB&#39;s <strong>joint-Laplace</strong> restricted log-likelihood: one Laplace approximation over the phylogenetic effects and both sets of fixed effects, with flat priors on the fixed effects. That is the quantity drmTMB&#39;s <code>REML = TRUE</code> maximises for this model. It is not the Patterson–Thompson quantity, because <code>beta_sigma</code> enters the likelihood non-linearly, so the fixed effects cannot be integrated out exactly. On the scale-only and separate blocks, <code>profile_ci = true</code> profiles the same restricted likelihood. The other variance parameters are re-optimised, and the fixed effects are integrated out rather than profiled. The coupled block (<code>phylo_coupled = true</code>) computes no profile interval.</p></div><h2 id="Model-comparison-and-accessors" tabindex="-1">Model comparison &amp; accessors <a class="header-anchor" href="#Model-comparison-and-accessors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Model comparison &amp; accessors {#Model-comparison-and-accessors}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>lrtest</code>, <code>anova</code>, <code>aicc</code>, <code>update</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>weights(fit)</code> and <code>drm(...; weights = w)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> on the fixed-effect ML routes of the univariate families (integer weights reproduce row duplication on all 16 routes, <code>test/test_lane_n3_drmcontrol.jl</code>); <code>weights(fit)</code> is <code>nothing</code> when none were given. Random-effect, structured, bivariate and REML routes refuse <code>weights =</code> by name (#931 tracks the rollout)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>drm(...; control = DRMControl(maxit, start, multi_start, se, optimizer))</code>, <code>gradient(fit)</code>, <code>hessian(fit)</code>, <code>control_info(fit)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>: <code>se = false</code> on every route; <code>maxit</code> / <code>start</code> / <code>multi_start</code> on fixed-effect univariate ML routes; direct <code>optimizer = :lbfgs</code> / <code>:bfgs</code> on those routes and Gaussian ML with one mean random intercept. Other random, structured, bivariate and REML routes refuse an explicit method; fit-based profile/bootstrap entrypoints refuse explicitly selected fits before refitting.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>aic</code> / <code>bic</code> / <code>dof</code> / <code>nobs</code> / <code>deviance</code> / <code>dof_residual</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>coef</code> / <code>vcov</code> / <code>confint</code> / <code>stderror</code> / <code>coeftable</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>fixef</code> / <code>re_sd</code> / <code>vc</code> / <code>ranef</code> / <code>sigma</code> / <code>corpairs</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>family</code> accessor</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>heritability</code> / <code>repeatability</code> / <code>icc</code> with delta + profile CIs</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Drop-in parity accessors (StatsAPI surface)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Prediction,-post-fit,-residuals,-simulation" tabindex="-1">Prediction, post-fit, residuals, simulation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Prediction,-post-fit,-residuals,-simulation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Prediction, post-fit, residuals, simulation {#Prediction,-post-fit,-residuals,-simulation}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>fitted</code> / <code>residuals</code> / response-scale <code>predict</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>predict_parameters</code> / <code>marginal_parameters</code> / <code>prediction_grid</code> / delta-method prediction SEs</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>simulate</code> / <code>check_drm</code> / Dunn–Smyth quantile residuals</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Visualization data (<code>profile_curve</code> / <code>parameter_surface</code> / <code>corpairs_data</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Drawing (<code>drm_figure</code> / thin <code>plot_*</code>; Confidence Eye on <code>:profile</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;">Optional with Makie and AlgebraOfGraphics; package checks cover the no-Makie fallback, not rendered figures</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="R-Julia-bridge-engine-&quot;julia&quot;" tabindex="-1">R → Julia bridge (engine = &quot;julia&quot;) <a class="header-anchor" href="#R-Julia-bridge-engine-&quot;julia&quot;" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;R → Julia bridge (engine = &quot;julia&quot;) {#R-Julia-bridge-engine-&quot;julia&quot;}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The optional R bridge for <code>drmTMB(..., engine = &quot;julia&quot;)</code> converts formulas and data into a form that Julia can fit, then converts the result back to R.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>drm_bridge</code> (string/dict/named-tuple formula → fit → flattened <code>Dict</code>); univariate, bivariate, phylo-mean, and narrow q2 structured Gaussian examples</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for the listed model configurations</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">q2/q4 direct-export helpers (<code>q2_point_export</code>, <code>q4_point_export</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for point summaries only; broader bridge behaviour and interval calibration have not been established</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>drm_bridge_inference</code> (profile + bootstrap)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for the named Gaussian cases in Current evidence boundaries below; these tests do not establish general bridge interval coverage.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Newick tree string parsing + small LRU cache</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Full R-side glue / <code>engine=&quot;julia&quot;</code> round-trip in drmTMB</td><td style="text-align:right;">(R repo) <strong>Not available here</strong>: the Julia primitive is tested; the R package glue lives in the drmTMB repository</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Marginal-method-selection-VA/ELBO" tabindex="-1">Marginal method selection (VA/ELBO) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Marginal-method-selection-VA/ELBO" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Marginal method selection (VA/ELBO) {#Marginal-method-selection-VA/ELBO}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Capability</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:LA</code>, the default integrator: per-group adaptive GHQ on an ordinary <code>(1|g)</code> on a non-Gaussian mean other than ZeroOneBeta (5 nodes per group; 21 for Tweedie, 41 for CumulativeLogit; a single node for LogNormal, where it is exact) and on non-Gaussian <code>(1 + x|g)</code> (5 nodes per axis, #834); a fixed 32-node grid (GHQ-32) on Gaussian <code>sigma ~ (1|g)</code> and ZeroOneBeta <code>(1|g)</code>; Laplace on most other random-effect structures</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:VA</code> Poisson <code>(1|g)</code> public path</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Experimental</strong>; it uses an ELBO approximation, labels the fit <code>:VA</code>, and refuses mixed LA/VA AIC or likelihood-ratio comparisons</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:VA</code> Binomial / NB2 / Gamma / Beta <code>(1|g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Experimental</strong>; scale families require <code>sigma ~ 1</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:Laplace</code> Poisson / Binomial / NB2 / Gamma / Beta <code>(1|g)</code> on the mean</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the TMB-convention Laplace approximation (the default <code>:LA</code> on this cell is per-group adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature, 5 nodes per group). Same log-likelihood as native drmTMB to ≤ 4e-10 on ten test datasets. Scale families require <code>sigma ~ 1</code>; ML only. Crossed terms, <code>sigma</code> covariates or random effects, <code>zi</code>/<code>hu</code> and REML are refused here; the slope, <code>sd()</code> and correlated-block shapes are in the next two rows. <code>lrtest</code> against the fixed-effects model works; against a <code>:LA</code> random-effect fit it is refused. The failures at a small family <code>sigma</code> (0.003 to 0.012) that made <code>:Laplace</code> the safer choice were measured under the fixed 32-node grid used before version 0.7.2 and have not been re-run against the adaptive default; with a family <code>sigma</code> that small, compare the two fits. Otherwise <code>:Laplace</code> is for parity with drmTMB, not a better default. With covariates in <code>sigma</code> (NB2, Gamma, Beta), <code>:Laplace</code> is refused and the default <code>:LA</code> log-likelihood is <strong>not comparable</strong> with drmTMB&#39;s Laplace log-likelihood (differences of 0.04 to 1.25 units on three real data sets, <a href="https://github.com/itchyshin/DRModels.jl/issues/943" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DRModels.jl#943</a>); compare estimates rather than log-likelihoods there</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:Laplace</code> Student <code>(1|g)</code> on the mean</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the TMB-convention Laplace approximation drmTMB uses (the default <code>:LA</code> on this cell is per-group adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature, 5 nodes per group). <code>sigma</code> and <code>nu</code> may carry fixed-effect formulas; ML only. A fixed-effects-only model, a correlated block <code>(1 + x|g)</code> and crossed terms (already fitted by Laplace by default) are refused; a single <code>(0 + x|g)</code> slope is accepted</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:Laplace</code> on the routes that default to quadrature: one <code>(1|g)</code> or <code>(0 + x|g)</code> with or without an <code>sd(g, coef(·)) ~ …</code> formula (Poisson, NegBinomial2, Binomial logit, Beta, Gamma, Student, Tweedie), independent terms with <code>sd()</code> formulas on Binomial, and one correlated block <code>(1 + x|g)</code> (Poisson and NegBinomial2 with two coefficients; Binomial with two to four, with or without <code>sd()</code> formulas)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> against drmTMB 0.8.1 on the committed gap-map data (<code>test/test_laplace_parity.jl</code>). The default is unchanged and stays the more accurate integral: adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature is closer to the exact marginal likelihood, whereas the Laplace approximation is what drmTMB reports, so the two log-likelihoods differ by 0.03 to 0.5 units on these data (Laplace is lower than exact for most families and higher for Poisson and Gamma slopes and for Tweedie). Pass <code>marginal = :Laplace</code> when you want drmTMB&#39;s number, for example to compare fits across the two packages. Do not compare a <code>:Laplace</code> and a default fit with <code>lrtest</code> or AIC. The correlated-block Binomial route is the same group integral with one adaptive node. Everything else with <code>sd()</code> (structured terms, <code>sigma</code> targets) is refused with <code>:Laplace</code>. A Binomial independent-term fit with an <code>sd(z)</code> formula can stop on a flat ridge below drmTMB&#39;s value (drmTMB flags its own fit non-converged there); Julia reports <code>converged = false</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>marginal=:Laplace</code> Gaussian <code>sigma ~ 1 + (1|g)</code> (random intercept on the scale axis, fixed-effect mean)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong>; the Laplace approximation native drmTMB uses (the default <code>:LA</code> is GHQ-32 on this cell). Same log-likelihood, estimates and standard errors as drmTMB on the test datasets; ML only. See the scale-axis row above for accuracy and for the remaining interval-coverage limit for the random-effect SD</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>method=:VA</code> on non-Gaussian <code>drm()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Rejected</strong> — choose <code>marginal=:VA</code>; <code>method</code> is ML/REML</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Absent-/-out-of-scope-explicit" tabindex="-1">Absent / out-of-scope (explicit) <a class="header-anchor" href="#Absent-/-out-of-scope-explicit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Absent / out-of-scope (explicit) {#Absent-/-out-of-scope-explicit}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>To avoid overclaiming, note these boundaries:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Missing data:</strong> listwise predictor preprocessing is not FIML. Experimental joint missing-predictor routes (<code>mi()</code>, <code>JointDrmFit</code>, <code>JointTwoDrmFit</code>, <code>JointFiniteDrmFit</code>, <code>imputed</code>, and <code>miss_control</code>) are exported for evaluation, but their API and numerics may change and they are not covered by R-parity evidence. Gaussian observed-response masking and missing-response handling for Location–Scale–Scale <code>sd()</code> models are available. <code>drm</code> drops rows with a <code>missing</code> predictor, and rows with a missing response on the Gaussian <code>(1|g)</code> and <code>meta_V</code> routes, with a warning that gives the count (R&#39;s <code>na.omit</code>, as drmTMB does; #1089). A bivariate fit (Gaussian, <code>LogNormal</code>, <code>Student</code>) drops a row when either response is missing, with a counted warning, as drmTMB&#39;s default <code>miss_control(response = &quot;drop&quot;)</code> does; <code>missing = miss_control(response = &quot;include&quot;)</code> keeps partly observed rows and fits their observed-response marginal likelihood (drmTMB implements <code>&quot;include&quot;</code> for the bivariate Gaussian only). General multiple imputation outside the joint-model routes and an <code>na.action</code> option to choose another behaviour are absent. See <a href="./model-guides/missing-data">Handling missing data</a> for what to do now.</p></li><li><p><strong>VA/ELBO:</strong> experimental random-intercept VA is limited to <code>(1|g)</code> for Poisson, Binomial, NB2, Gamma, and Beta (<code>sigma ~ 1</code> where applicable). Phylogenetic, crossed, correlated-slope, and zero-inflated/hurdle variants are not available.</p></li><li><p><strong>Experimental prototypes:</strong> experimental optimisation and diagnostic prototypes are not public analysis methods. The supported REML and <code>algorithm = :em</code> routes are listed in the Inference table.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Current-evidence-boundaries" tabindex="-1">Current evidence boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#Current-evidence-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Current evidence boundaries {#Current-evidence-boundaries}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>Retained <code>drm_bridge_inference</code> tests cover the Gaussian phylogenetic SD block (<code>param=:resd</code>), a fixed-effect coefficient profile/bootstrap, bivariate q4 phylogenetic SD intervals, and the sigma-phylogenetic SD (<code>resd_sigma</code>) route. These are named test cases, not a claim that every bridge parameter or model has qualified intervals.</p></li><li><p>Coupled q=2 location–scale fitting is public and tested for NB2 and Gamma. Beta and beta-binomial cannot currently be requested through <code>drm()</code> and <code>bf()</code> for this model. Poisson and LogNormal versions are implemented but have not yet been tested as complete analyses.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Formula-shapes-that-differ-from-R" tabindex="-1">Formula shapes that differ from R <a class="header-anchor" href="#Formula-shapes-that-differ-from-R" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Formula shapes that differ from R {#Formula-shapes-that-differ-from-R}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These came out of the second R-Julia gap map (2026-11-09). Each row was run against drmTMB 0.8.1.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">R spelling</th><th style="text-align:right;">Julia</th><th style="text-align:right;">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(f | g)</code>, <code>(1 + f | g)</code>, <code>(0 + f | g)</code> with a factor <code>f</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Refused with an <code>ArgumentError</code> (drmTMB refuses too). Use a numeric 0/1 column for one level.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>zi ~ 1 + (1 | g)</code>, <code>hu ~ … + (1 | g)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Refused with an <code>ArgumentError</code> (drmTMB refuses too). Put the random effect on <code>mu</code>.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> for <code>zi</code>; the <code>hu</code> refusal was checked by hand for NegBinomial2 and TruncatedNegBinomial2, not by a test</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>(1 + x || g)</code> (double bar)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>@formula</code> cannot parse <code>||</code>. Write <code>(1 | g) + (0 + x | g)</code>: same log-likelihood as drmTMB on the committed fixture.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (spelling)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>poly(x, 2)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>@formula</code> has no <code>poly</code>. Write a function term such as <code>x + sq(x)</code> (same ML log-likelihood, different coefficients); the string route <code>engine = &quot;julia&quot;</code> reproduces R&#39;s orthogonal basis.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong> (spelling)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Ordinal response as labels (<code>&quot;o1&quot;</code>…<code>&quot;o4&quot;</code>)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>CumulativeLogit()</code> codes string or symbol labels 1..K in sorted label order; pass integer codes for another order. drmTMB refuses a character response and an unordered factor, so this is a Julia convenience, and the R reference uses an ordered factor with the same level order.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Rows with a missing grouping label</td><td style="text-align:right;">Refused (#1112); <code>drm_listwise(formula, data)</code> drops them and then agrees with drmTMB, which drops those rows.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>weights</code> with a missing response</td><td style="text-align:right;">Weights follow their rows; one weight per row of <code>data</code>.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Cross-family pair containing <code>LogNormal()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Refused with the list of supported families. drmTMB refuses every mixed-response pair, including the supported ones, so there is no R comparison for this refusal.</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>Tested</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><hr><p><em>“Tested” supports the listed use, not a package-wide performance or interval-coverage claim. Always assess your fitted model and study design.</em></p>',74)])])}const u=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
